# DIAGNOSTICS

A command that cannot be done says why, after `dbg:`, such as `dbg: no such function or line`, `dbg: not running` or `dbg: no stack`; an expression that cannot be evaluated says why, such as `dbg: no such variable` or `dbg: variable not available here`.

The exit string is `usage`, `no symbols` when the program's ELF cannot be read or indexed, `cannot read the script`, `path too long`, or `no namespace`; it is empty otherwise, whatever the commands met.

# BUGS

Only the command line exists, without the graphical debugger of the design, view rules, watch expressions, disassembly, or stepping by source line, over or out.
`step` steps one instruction; on x86_64 a step over `syscall` stops one instruction late.

The ELF is read and indexed each time `dbg` starts, and must be at most 8 MiB; symbols are not looked for by build ID, and an attached process or a crash directory must name a program in `/boot/bin`.
A crash directory is recognised only by its path's starting `/tmp/crash/`, and is opened at its thread 1, whichever thread faulted.

There is no `stop`: an attached process can be stopped only by a breakpoint.
`step` steps only a thread stopped at an event; the rest stay stopped meanwhile, frozen by procfs(4), and a thread one made while they were stopped runs.
After a launched program ends, `run` refuses to start it again.
`dbg` prints no exit string for an attached process that ends, since it has no wait record for it.

The index has no inlined frames: an inlined function shows as its caller, and variables of optimised code may be `not available here`.
The call stack is followed by frame pointers alone, so code built without them shows a short stack.
Floating-point literals and arithmetic are not supported, though floating-point values print.
Breakpoints with conditions and counts, and watchpoints, which procfs(4) has, cannot be set from `dbg`.
